---
<SiteLayout title="Qwen3.8 Flash Next on AMD Strix Halo, Windows | Halowin Bubo" description="Run Qwen3.8 Flash Next on an AMD Strix Halo laptop under Windows. See measured speeds, supported models and what you need. Open source, MIT license.">
  <span class="eyebrow">Native Windows · AMD HIP</span>
  <h1>Halowin Bubo</h1>
  <p class="intro">General-purpose tools such as LM Studio, Ollama and llama.cpp aim at every GPU. Halowin Bubo is built for one chip and one model, and it runs natively on Windows with AMD HIP. This page covers what it runs, how fast it is and what you need. The <a href="/">home page</a> has the short version.</p>
  <div class="cta-row"><a class="button" href="https://github.com/haloforge-labs/bubo/releases/tag/v0.1.0">Download v0.1.0 for Windows</a><a class="cta-link" href="/docs/system-setup/">System setup <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div>
  <div class="article-grid">
    <aside class="side-box" aria-label="Requirements and download">
      <h2>You need</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Windows 11</li>
        <li>An AMD Strix Halo machine with 128 GB of memory, with 96 GB set as GPU memory and a 128 GB page file</li>
        <li>The Microsoft Visual C++ Redistributable (x64)</li>
        <li>About 121 GB of free disk for the model files</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Other 128 GB machines with the same chip may work. We make no promise.</p>
      <a class="button side-download" href="https://github.com/haloforge-labs/bubo/releases/tag/v0.1.0">Download v0.1.0</a>
      <p class="side-docs"><a href="/docs/">Read the documentation <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></p>
    </aside>
    <div class="article-body">
      <section aria-labelledby="for"><h2 id="for">For your Windows laptop</h2><p>Bubo is built and tested for AMD Strix Halo machines with 128 GB of memory that run Windows, mostly laptops. It can work on other machines, but we make no promise.</p><p>It is tuned for Ryzen AI Max laptops with Radeon 8060S graphics, Qwen3.8 Flash Next in GGUF format with the UD-Q4_K_XL quant, and Performance mode. <a href="/docs/system-setup/">Set up your system</a> before you install it.</p></section>
      <section aria-labelledby="speed">
        <h2 id="speed">Measured speed</h2>
        <p>Every number here comes from our own tests on one laptop. The <a href="/docs/benchmarks/#test-conditions">test conditions</a> list the hardware, the model and the dates.</p>
        <Measurements />
        <p class="benchmark-comparison">With several users at once, Bubo with MTP reaches 37.4 tok/s for one user and 79.2 for four. At eight users, plain decode reaches 102.2 tok/s in total. Each user has 4K of context. The one-user rate is ahead of upstream gufo’s published one-user MTP rate, and the four-user rate is level with it. Bubo runs on a laptop while upstream’s numbers come from full-power hardware. <a href="/docs/benchmarks/#many-users">See the table</a>.</p>
        <p class="benchmark-comparison">On 35 GPQA, AIME, HMMT and IMO requests, Bubo solved 29 and gufo with pixmaate’s Windows port solved 27, and Bubo decoded about 24% faster. The sets are small, so read this as no loss of accuracy. <a href="/docs/benchmarks/">See the table</a>.</p>
        <a href="/docs/benchmarks/">Read the benchmark details <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="models"><h2 id="models">Supported models</h2><p>Bubo keeps every model family of gufo: DeepSeek V4 Flash, Qwen3.8 27B, Qwen3.8 Flash Next, Qwen3.6 35B-A3B, Qwen3-ASR 1.7B, Qwen3-TTS 1.7B, Qwen-Image-2.1 and MiniMax H3. It is tuned for Qwen3.8 Flash Next, which has 125B parameters with 6B active, plus a 51B n-gram embedding and a 4B MTP head. The other models run with upstream gufo settings.</p><p>The default model is an abliterated build of Qwen3.8 Flash Next from Huihui, which refuses less than the original. The benchmarks on this site use it, and the twin and the draft head match it. Read the usage warnings on its model card. The plain Qwen shards also run, without the twin and the draft head.</p><p>The default profile trades exact output for speed: it accepts near-miss drafts in thinking text. <code>--profile exact</code> matched the stock reference token for token in our test.</p><a href="/docs/models/">See the model table <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></section>
      <section aria-labelledby="downloads"><h2 id="downloads">Downloads</h2><p><a href="https://github.com/haloforge-labs/bubo/releases/tag/v0.1.0">Halowin Bubo v0.1.0 for Windows (zip and SHA-256) on GitHub</a>. This is the first release, so read the <a href="/docs/known-issues/">known issues</a> first.</p></section>
      <section aria-labelledby="license"><h2 id="license">License and credit</h2><p>Bubo is open source under the <a href="https://github.com/haloforge-labs/bubo/blob/main/LICENSE">MIT license</a>, the same license as gufo. The code is copyright HaloForge Labs and the gufo contributors.</p><p>Bubo is built on <a href="https://github.com/gufo-org/gufo">gufo</a>. Credit goes to the gufo authors for the upstream server and to pixmaate for the gufo Windows port.</p><p>The model files keep the Qwen Community License 1.0, as the <a href="https://huggingface.co/HaloForgeLabs/bubo-qwen3.8-flash-next">model card</a> states. Third-party notices are in <a href="https://github.com/haloforge-labs/bubo/blob/main/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md">THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md</a>.</p></section>
    </div>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
